{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/round76-brief.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council round 76: review the slice-2 design (the reader's page) before any code (read-only)\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are an independent reviewer. Do not edit any file. The repository is /Users/lchingen/hivemind. Cite file:line.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What to review\n     6\t\n     7\t`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md`, the design for slice 2 of the \"Forecast unavailable\" release. Slice 1 is\n     8\tbuilt and accepted (D759 at the end of `docs/DECISIONS-log.md`; `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11; `RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`;\n     9\t`src/hivemind/release.py`, `scripts/release_build.py`, `tests/test_release.py`). D756 asks for a $0 check of the reader's screen on\n    10\ta stored number question with a forecast and history: only the question, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear. D758\n    11\tordered the exporter first, the screen second. Council 74 deferred idea submission.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Questions\n    14\t\n    15\t1. **Leaks.** Can anything other than the package data reach the reader's screen, or any network request leave the page? Consider:\n    16\t   the CSP, inline script and style hashes, `file://` origin rules, links, `<details>`, `noscript`, the escaping of the embedded JSON.\n    17\t2. **The page = `render_page(data)` rule.** Is that the right structural check for the page? Is it sound with a read-once package? Is\n    18\t   the format bump to `hivemind-release/2` the right handling?\n    19\t3. **The screen check (§6).** Will CDP on headless Chrome show a CSP-blocked fetch as a request or a violation? Is the v8 screen\n    20\t   assertion set right, and literal enough (council 75d's rule)? Is the budget set before the control?\n    21\t4. **The tables (§2–§4) and tests (§5).** Is any cell blank or wrong? Is any test unable to fail without its code?\n    22\t5. **Scope.** Is anything in this design scope drift beyond D756/D758, or a change to the POC?\n    23\t6. **Verdict:** ready to build, or not ready with each serious finding.\n    24\t\n    25\t**Out of scope:**\n    26\t- the frozen judging, opening and extraction pieces;\n    27\t- the closed v13s line;\n    28\t- slice 1's accepted internals, unless slice 2 breaks them;\n    29\t- the idea path;\n    30\t- the recorded evidence-summary disagreement;\n    31\t- wording.\n    32\t\n    33\tReport only findings that change what is built or whether it is safe.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r76d.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t**Not ready to build.** Revision 4 closes both corrections from round 76c. One screen-check gap remains. The check can accept Morningstar’s full quote even when the reader can see only its first line. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:197](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:197) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:211](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:211)\n     2\t\n     3\t**1. Both 76c corrections are closed.**\n     4\t\n     5\t- **Extra forecast beside a card.** The check now reads the whole page. Text outside the expected header, cards and evidence groups fails. The stylesheet may generate no text, and the design requires a copy with an extra forecast immediately before `v8` to fail. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:147](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:147) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:200](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:200) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:226)\n     6\t- **Transparent list passing the timer.** The timer now requires every expected card to pass the same opacity-aware visibility check used for the evidence. A fully transparent list must miss the three-second deadline. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:197](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:197) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:227](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:227) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:252](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:252)\n     7\t\n     8\t**2. One serious finding remains.** The check reads a text node’s entire value once its element passes `checkVisibility` and has a nonzero box. A quote clipped to one line still meets both conditions. In the accepted `v8` package, Morningstar’s quote is 219 characters and its estimate comes late in the sentence. At phone width, the reader could lose the estimate while the full-text comparison and the page-width check both pass. The saved screenshot covers only the card’s top. This is a design finding. Slice 2 has no browser implementation to test yet. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:198](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:198) · [release_check.py:174](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/release_check.py:174) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:215](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:215) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:247](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:247) · [CSS visibility algorithm](https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#dom-element-checkvisibility)\n     9\t\n    10\t**3. One correction before build.** Require expanded quotes to wrap and remain fully readable at 390 px. Use the stored Morningstar entry as the test. The normal page must show its full quote when scrolled to it. A copy that clips the quote to one line must fail even though its text is identical. I found no separate forecast leak, outgoing request path or other build blocker in the specified design. [RELEASE-PAGE.md:70](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:70) · [RELEASE-PAGE.md:211](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md:211)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-PAGE.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release, slice 2: the reader's page (design for council round 76)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: revision 5, for council round 76f. No code yet.**\n     4\t\n     5\t**Revision 5 answers `codex-r76d.md`:** no text may be clipped.\n     6\t- The template's stylesheet uses none of `overflow`, `clip`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height` or `nowrap`.\n     7\t- In the browser, every block element holding read text shows all of it. This is checked at desktop width and at 390 px, with the\n     8\t  stored Morningstar entry as the test.\n     9\t- A clipping copy is added as a negative page.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Revision 4 answers `codex-r76c.md`:**\n    12\t- the screen check compares the whole page's visible text, header and every card, with a frozen list; any text outside a known place\n    13\t  fails;\n    14\t- visibility is one test, `checkVisibility` with opacity and visibility, for the evidence and for the timer;\n    15\t- the template's stylesheet draws no text (no `content`);\n    16\t- a sibling forecast line and a fully transparent list are added as negative pages.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Revision 3 answers `codex-r76b.md`:**\n    19\t- the screen check compares every visible text of each checked card with a frozen list, in order and grouped by evidence, so an\n    20\t  extra line fails;\n    21\t- each dated card is frozen from its own dated package;\n    22\t- the timer stops when the list is visible;\n    23\t- the dark background is the literal `rgb(15, 20, 13)`.\n    24\t\n    25\tRevision 2 answered `codex-r76.md`:\n    26\t- source addresses are plain text, never links;\n    27\t- the escaping is written out as literal JSON escapes, with the charset and the CSP first in the file;\n    28\t- the format-1 checks are updated, and a lone page-hash error has its own test;\n    29\t- the screen check compares the expanded `v8` card with frozen expected entries;\n    30\t- the fetch mutant keeps its script running and must name `connect-src` and its destination;\n    31\t- the fallback, timer, phone-width and dark-scheme conditions are made observable;\n    32\t- the text filter is dropped. Slice 1 is built and accepted (D759: `RELEASE-BUILD.md` revision 11,\n    33\t`RELEASE-CHECK-RESULT.md`). Council 73 (D758) ordered the exporter first and the reader's screen second. This slice completes D756's\n    34\tscreen check: a $0 check of the reader's screen on a stored number question that has a forecast and history, where only the\n    35\tquestion, its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\" appear.\n    36\t\n    37\t## 0. Scope\n    38\t\n    39\t- **Built:**\n    40\t  - a page that is a pure function of the package data;\n    41\t  - the package gains that page (format 2);\n    42\t  - the package check proves the page equals that function of the data;\n    43\t  - a headless-browser check of the `old-1/v8` screen.\n    44\t- **The page reads only the package's data,** embedded in it.\n    45\t  - No network request of any kind: no live POC service, no `/library` fetch, no translated or prepared JSON, no composite trials,\n    46\t    no fonts or images from outside.\n    47\t  - No idea submission control or route (council 74).\n    48\t- **Not in scope:**\n    49\t  - the POC's page, API and MCP, unchanged;\n    50\t  - colleagues' page;\n    51\t  - the idea path;\n    52\t  - any model call;\n    53\t  - commit, publish, deploy (Alex's).\n    54\t\n    55\t**Why a separate page, not the POC's library UI.** The POC's components read the `thesis-ui/2` contract, which needs a ladder,\n    56\tscores and judgements for every thesis; a number question in the package has none. Adapting those shared components would change\n    57\tthe POC, which stays unchanged (design §2, D757). A small page written for the package has nothing to adapt and nothing that can\n    58\treach the network.\n    59\t\n    60\t## 1. What is built\n    61\t\n    62\t**A visible fallback.** The page starts with the line \"This release page needs JavaScript to show its questions.\" The script removes it\n    63\tonce the list is built. A browser that blocks the inline script keeps that line (`<noscript>` would stay hidden there; council\n    64\t76).\n    65\t\n    66\t**In `src/hivemind/release.py`:**\n    67\t- **`FORMAT = 'hivemind-release/2'`.** The data's allow-list (`release-allow-list/1`) is unchanged.\n    68\t- **`PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json', 'index.html'}`.** The manifest's closed set gains `page_sha256`.\n    69\t- **`render_page(data_bytes) -> bytes`:** one fixed template, the module constant `PAGE_TEMPLATE`, with the data inserted once.\n    70\t  - **The data** goes into `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"release-data\">`, written from the exact `release-data.json` bytes,\n    71\t    with each of these characters replaced by its six-character JSON escape: `<` by `\\u003c`, `>` by `\\u003e`, `&` by `\\u0026`,\n    72\t    U+2028 by `\\u2028`, U+2029 by `\\u2029`. These replacements stay valid JSON and parse back to the same data. The block can then\n    73\t    hold no `</script` and no `<!--`.\n    74\t  - **The file starts** with `<!doctype html>`, then `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the CSP `<meta>`, before any style, script or\n    75\t    data.\n    76\t  - **A Content-Security-Policy `<meta>`:**\n    77\t    `default-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-<inline script>'; style-src 'sha256-<inline style>'; img-src 'none'; font-src 'none';\n    78\t    connect-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'`. The two hashes are computed from the template's own script and style,\n    79\t    so the browser refuses any other script, style or connection.\n    80\t  - **The script** builds the page only with `document.createElement`, `textContent` and `className`. It creates no `<a>` element,\n    81\t    no `href` and no `src`. It never uses `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write`, `eval`, `Function`, or any network API\n    82\t    (`fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `EventSource`, `navigator.sendBeacon`, `import()`, `Worker`).\n    83\t  - **The style** uses system fonts and inline colour tokens copied from `web/design-system/tokens.css`. It generates no text: no\n    84\t    `content` property anywhere, so every word a reader sees is a text node the screen check can read. It clips no text: none of\n    85\t    `overflow`, `clip`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height` or `white-space: nowrap`, and long words wrap\n    86\t    (`overflow-wrap: anywhere`). It has a dark scheme under\n    87\t    `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, and it works at phone width.\n    88\t- **`write_package`** also writes `index.html = render_page(data bytes)` and records its sha256.\n    89\t- **`check_package`** also:\n    90\t  - reads `index.html` once through the same folder handle;\n    91\t  - requires its bytes to equal `render_page(the data bytes it read)` exactly;\n    92\t  - requires the manifest's `page_sha256` to match.\n    93\t\n    94\t  A page that differs by one byte from the function of the data is refused. So a page cannot carry anything the data does not hold,\n    95\t  and no code the template does not hold.\n    96\t\n    97\t**What the page shows,** in package order. Each shown value sits in an element with a fixed class, so the screen check can read it:\n    98\t`question-text`, `question-status`, `question-forecast`, `question-answer`, `evidence-count`, `evidence-summary`, `evidence-quote`,\n    99\t`evidence-meta`, `source-title`, `source-publisher`, `source-day`, `source-address`.\n   100\t- **A header:** \"Hivemind release\", the package's day, and the count of questions by class.\n   101\t- **Each question:**\n   102\t  - its question text and \"Open\" or \"Closed\";\n   103\t  - for a number question, the marker \"Forecast unavailable\";\n   104\t  - for an event or Yes/No question, \"Answer: <label>\", or \"Not yet answered\" when the answer is null;\n   105\t  - \"<n> pieces of evidence\", and a `<details>` element. When opened, it builds the evidence list:\n   106\t    - each piece shows its summary, the quote, and a line with tier, speaker, and original speaker and day;\n   107\t    - each source shows title, publisher, day and its address as plain text. The page has no links, so neither a click nor a script\n   108\t      can make a request (council 76).\n   109\t\n   110\t  Evidence is built only when opened, so the first screen stays fast on a 6 MB package.\n   111\t\n   112\t## 2. State × event (the page and the check)\n   113\t\n   114\t| State / event | What happens |\n   115\t|---|---|\n   116\t| Package with no questions | \"No questions as of <day>\" |\n   117\t| A question with no evidence | \"No evidence in force\", and no `<details>` |\n   118\t| A source address of any scheme, `javascript:` included | shown as text; the page has no links |\n   119\t| Text holding `</script>`, `<img onerror=…>`, `&` or U+2028 | the embed escapes it, the page shows it as text, and the JSON parses back identical |\n   120\t| The page is opened from disk, a static host or any path | it reads only its embedded data; nothing else exists to read |\n   121\t| The browser blocks the inline script | the fallback line stays visible; nothing else renders, and the page holds no forecast to leak |\n   122\t| A page edited after the build (a fetch added, a field added) | `check_package` refuses: the bytes differ from `render_page(data)` |\n   123\t| The template changes in a later version | old packages fail `check_package` under the new code by design; a new template is a new format version |\n   124\t| A dark scheme and a phone width | checked in §6: at 390 px wide the page has no horizontal scroll; with the dark scheme emulated, the body's background is the dark token |\n   125\t\n   126\t## 3. Value × reader\n   127\t\n   128\t- Every value the page shows comes from the embedded data; the page reads no other value.\n   129\t- The page's readers are a person's browser and the browser check.\n   130\t- The data's readers are unchanged from slice 1: `check_package`, `check_origins`, the tests. The page adds one reader of the data,\n   131\t  the page script, and no new writer.\n   132\t\n   133\t## 4. New path × old checks\n   134\t\n   135\t| Slice-1 check | Slice 2 |\n   136\t|---|---|\n   137\t| Closed set of the data, origin check, staged check before the no-replace publication, the no-replace report | unchanged |\n   138\t| The manifest's closed set | gains `page_sha256`; `format` becomes `hivemind-release/2` |\n   139\t| Exactly the package files, each read once, no links, no pipes | the same rule over three files |\n   140\t| The literal-values rule of verification (council 75d) | applied to the page checks as well |\n   141\t| `release_check.py` pins `hivemind-release/1`; its helpers that forge data refresh only the data hash | the pin becomes `/2`. The forging helpers also regenerate `index.html = render_page(forged data)` and its hash, so a forged forecast still reaches the store comparison and is refused there, with its exact reason |\n   142\t\n   143\t## 5. Tests (`tests/test_release.py`) and mutants\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Determinism.** `render_page` of the fixture data twice gives the same bytes.\n   146\t2. **Escaping.**\n   147\t   - A fixture quote holding `</script><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`, an `&` and a U+2028 is embedded with the literal escapes.\n   148\t   - The page bytes hold no `</script` inside the data block and no `<!--`.\n   149\t   - Parsing the block back gives the data unchanged.\n   150\t   - In the browser check (§6), on the fixture page with that evidence expanded, the exact quote is visible as text, and the page has\n   151\t     no `img` element.\n   152\t3. **No network code, no drawn text.**\n   153\t   - The template's script contains none of the listed APIs, and no `createElement('a')`, `href` or `src`.\n   154\t   - Its CSP is exactly the policy above, with the hashes of its own script and style.\n   155\t   - Its stylesheet has no `content` property, and none of `overflow`, `clip`, `text-overflow`, `line-clamp`, `max-height`,\n   156\t     `nowrap`.\n   157\t4. **The package check:**\n   158\t   - a page changed by one byte, with the manifest hash refreshed, is refused with its exact reason;\n   159\t   - a page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added, its CSP hash and manifest hash both updated, is refused, because it\n   160\t     is not `render_page(data)`;\n   161\t   - **a correct page with only `page_sha256` wrong is refused with its exact reason**, which catches a deleted hash check;\n   162\t   - a missing page and a fourth file are each refused;\n   163\t   - all three files are read once through the folder handle, and the page bytes compared are the bytes hashed.\n   164\t5. **Literal identifiers:** `hivemind-release/2` and `release-allow-list/1`, in the tests and the acceptance script.\n   165\t\n   166\t**Mutants:**\n   167\t- `render_page` not escaping `<`;\n   168\t- the script using `innerHTML` for the quote;\n   169\t- a source address rendered as a link;\n   170\t- `check_package` not comparing the page with `render_page`;\n   171\t- `check_package` not checking `page_sha256`;\n   172\t- the CSP hash computed over a different script.\n   173\t\n   174\t## 6. The $0 screen check (`release_screen.mjs`, run once)\n   175\t\n   176\t- **Setup.** Headless Chrome from `/Applications/Google Chrome.app`, driven through its DevTools protocol with Node's built-in\n   177\t  WebSocket, on a fresh profile in the scratchpad. Each page is opened from `file://`.\n   178\t- **Packages:** `old-1` at its default day, and the two dated leak packages (`old-3` at 2026-08-25, `old-1` at 2026-08-28), built by\n   179\t  the slice-1 command under format 2.\n   180\t\n   181\t**Recorded for each page:**\n   182\t- the page's own network requests (`Network.requestWillBeSent` for the page target). Only the document itself may appear;\n   183\t- console errors, exceptions and CSP violations (`Runtime.exceptionThrown`, `Log.entryAdded`). Zero are allowed.\n   184\t\n   185\t**Observation.** The `Network`, `Log` and `Runtime` domains are enabled before navigation and stay enabled through every expansion.\n   186\tAny of these fails the page:\n   187\t- a request other than the document;\n   188\t- a `Log.entryAdded` error;\n   189\t- a `Runtime.exceptionThrown`;\n   190\t- a `Runtime.consoleAPICalled` of type `error`.\n   191\t\n   192\t**Expected values, frozen before the run.** `release_screen.mjs` reads `screen-expected.json`. It is written before the first screen\n   193\trun, with its sha256 recorded in the result, from the accepted slice-1 packages:\n   194\t- `poc/data/release-check-2026-11-23g/old-1/package` for `v8`;\n   195\t- `old-3/package-2026-08-25` for `old-3/v36` (at that day it asks whether NVIDIA will begin shipping Rubin Ultra);\n   196\t- `old-1/package-2026-08-28` for `old-1/v10`.\n   197\t\n   198\tFor each checked page it holds the page's complete visible text:\n   199\t- **the header:** \"Hivemind release\", \"As of <day>\", and the counts line;\n   200\t- **every card collapsed,** in package order: its id, then its question, status, the marker or the answer, and the evidence count\n   201\t  (or \"No evidence in force\");\n   202\t- **the checked card expanded:** for each evidence entry in order, its own group: summary, quote, meta line, and per source its\n   203\t  title, publisher, day and address.\n   204\t\n   205\t**How the page is read.**\n   206\t- **What counts as visible.** The check walks every text node of `document.body` in document order. It keeps a text only when its\n   207\t  element passes `checkVisibility({opacityProperty: true, visibilityProperty: true, contentVisibilityAuto: true})` and has a\n   208\t  non-zero box. A text inside a transparent, hidden or collapsed ancestor is not visible.\n   209\t- **Not clipped.** For each kept text whose element is a block (not `display: inline`), the element's `scrollWidth` is at most\n   210\t  `clientWidth + 1` and its `scrollHeight` at most `clientHeight + 1`. Any clipped text fails the page, whatever its content.\n   211\t- **Where each text goes:** to the header, to a card (by `data-id`), to an evidence group within a card, or else to `other`.\n   212\t  `other` must be empty.\n   213\t- **What must match.** The read page must equal the frozen page exactly: collapsed, then with the checked card expanded, then after\n   214\t  closing and reopening it.\n   215\t- **What that catches.** An extra visible line anywhere fails: inside the card, beside it, or in the header. For example,\n   216\t  \"Forecast: $361 billion\", Morningstar's genuine figure shown as if it were the system's. So does a missing, duplicated, regrouped\n   217\t  or reordered entry.\n   218\t\n   219\t**The screen of `old-1/v8`:**\n   220\t- **Collapsed:** the whole read page equals the frozen page. The `v8` card reads \"How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA\n   221\t  report for FY2027?\", \"Open\", \"Forecast unavailable\", \"1284 pieces of evidence\".\n   222\t- **With `v8` expanded:** the whole read page equals the frozen page with `v8`'s 1,284 groups. The full Morningstar quote and its\n   223\t  attribution are included, and \"Bullish\" appears only where a frozen source text holds it.\n   224\t- **Closed and reopened once:** the same.\n   225\t- **No link anywhere on the page:** zero `a` elements, zero `[href]`, zero `[src]`.\n   226\t- **A screenshot** of the expanded card's top is saved with the result.\n   227\t\n   228\t**The leak screens.** For `old-3/v36` at 2026-08-25 and `old-1/v10` at 2026-08-28:\n   229\t- the page's day equals its package's;\n   230\t- the whole read page, collapsed and with the card expanded, equals the frozen page from that dated package. The card holds its\n   231\t  question, status, \"Answer: Yes\", \"1 piece of evidence\", then its single evidence entry and source;\n   232\t- so the entry is visible, and nothing else is: neither judgement's explanation, nor \"around $1 trillion\", \"rung 4\" or \"at rung\".\n   233\t\n   234\t**The decisive negatives** (checks of the check). Each is a copy of the `old-1` page with its script changed and its CSP hash\n   235\trecomputed, so the changed script runs. Each must fail for its own reason:\n   236\t- **inside the card:** one visible line \"Forecast: $361 billion\" added in the `v8` card fails the comparison;\n   237\t- **beside the card:** the same line added just before the `v8` card, as its sibling, fails the comparison (it lands in `other`);\n   238\t- **transparent:** the whole list set to `opacity: 0` misses the 3 s deadline, because no card is visible, although every card\n   239\t  exists;\n   240\t- **clipped:** quotes held to one line (`white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis` added through the script's\n   241\t  inline style on each quote) fail the not-clipped rule at 390 px, although their text is identical.\n   242\t\n   243\t**The escaping screen.** The fixture page of test 2, with that evidence expanded, shows the exact quote as text and has no `img`\n   244\telement.\n   245\t\n   246\t**The fetch mutant.**\n   247\t- **Setup:** a copy of the `old-1` page with `fetch(\"http://127.0.0.1:59999/library\")` added to its script, and the CSP script hash\n   248\t  recomputed for the altered script, so the script runs.\n   249\t- **It must fail the check for the right reason:**\n   250\t  - the normal list renders;\n   251\t  - a `Log.entryAdded` security error names `http://127.0.0.1:59999/library` and `connect-src`.\n   252\t\n   253\t  A page whose script is blocked wholesale does not earn the credit.\n   254\t- **Measured on this Mac (Chrome 158, headless, scratchpad probe):** a CSP-blocked fetch produces no `Network.requestWillBeSent`. It\n   255\t  produces a `security` error naming the address and the directive, and a `javascript` error.\n   256\t- **The probe page named port 8788.** Chrome refused it under the CSP before connecting (\"Refused to connect\"), so no request reached\n   257\t  that service. Port 59999 is not on the fetch specification's blocked-port list, and nothing listens there.\n   258\t\n   259\t**Phone width and dark scheme.**\n   260\t- **At 390 × 844** (`Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride`):\n   261\t  - `document.scrollingElement.scrollWidth` is at most 390;\n   262\t  - with `v8` expanded, the whole read page, with the not-clipped rule, equals the frozen expanded page;\n   263\t  - Morningstar's quote (`e2295`), scrolled into view, wraps over more than one line (`getClientRects` on its text), and its box lies\n   264\t    within the 390 px viewport.\n   265\t- With `Emulation.setEmulatedMedia` `prefers-color-scheme: dark`, the body's computed background is exactly `rgb(15, 20, 13)` (the\n   266\t  shared `--ink`, used as the dark page colour). In light, it is `rgb(245, 242, 235)` (`--paper`).\n   267\t\n   268\t**Budget,** set before the first control:\n   269\t- at most 3 s for the `old-1` page in headless Chrome, from `Page.navigate` until the full list is visible: the fallback line is\n   270\t  gone, and every expected question's card is present and passes the same visibility test as the text;\n   271\t- the page's size at most the data's bytes plus 64 KB.\n   272\t\n   273\t## 7. Prior decisions\n   274\t\n   275\tD726, D756, D757, D758, D759; council 74 (no idea input); council 75d (literal expected values); D690 (poc-v118 stays the product);\n   276\tD163 (English product text).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -lc docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,70p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"     201   26537 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n    1239  137574 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\n     492   54029 AGENTS.md\n    1932  218140 total\n# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n\nAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n\n| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n\n## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
